Section B

    Opportunity cost is defined as the cost of choosing one thing to do, measured in
    the loss of another good that you would need to give up. For example, the
    opportunity cost of producing 250,000 coffee beans is the other agricultural
    products that you aren't producing. This is because you instead choose to allocate 
    resources that could be used for agricultural products and instead use these resources 
    for coffee beans.

    In 2021, frost destroyed approximately 20% of Brazil's coffee crops, causing
    global coffee prices to surge by 70%. 

    This means that, by diversifying into other products, Brazil could facilitate
    more trade by allowing for other countries to produce more coffee, whilst also
    lowering the risk of having their economy damaged by the hit to coffee
    production. Therefore, the opportunity cost of producing mostly coffee is the
    economic safety that is being sacrificed.

    However, it could be worse to diversify, because there is also a risk of the
    products that Brazil is able to produce being better made by other countries e.g.
    if Brazil wanted to expand into the manufacturing market, most people would not
    look to go to Brazil in favor of China, which may offer cheaper and more reliable
    sources of manufacturing.


    The opportunity cost of maintaining the coffee sector is also quite high. If
    Brazil still focused mainly on coffee, but also diversified to make room for
    education and other government resources, it would allow the country to grow more
    in the education sector, and give them more influence on academia worldwide.

    Additionally, by maintaining their current methods of production, Brazil leaves
    itself open to rapidly changing consumer tastes and climate shocks, which is
    increasingly relevant today. By transitioning to another solution, like specialty
    coffee, Brazil will allow itself to transition to a more lucrative business while
    also giving itself more time and resources to allocate to another business, or
    even education.

    However, by switching away from coffee production, Brazil also risks losing many
    people who are employed due to the coffee business. The number currently sits at
    2 million people directly, with millions more in related industries.



